// Sub-plan D fixture: the width solver reaches a fixpoint over backward
// edges.
//
// A backward branch is an edge from later in the instruction stream to
// earlier, so a loop head can be reached at one width on the first arrival
// and another on the back edge. A single forward traversal never sees the
// second arrival: it assembles `agreeing` correctly by luck and claims a
// width at `diverging`'s head that only one of its two predecessors supports.
//
// The two functions differ only in the direction of the mode change inside
// the loop.

// The head is entered at @a8 and closed from @a16, so no width holds there.
// Nothing width-dependent may be written at the head — a `lda #$01` there is
// `K816-E0104`, which `tests/width.rs` pins — and `nop` does not care, so the
// rest of the function still assembles.
func diverging @a8 {
.head:
    nop
    rep #$20
    bra .head
}

// The back edge agrees with the entry contract, so the head stays 8-bit and
// the `lda #$01` there is the two-byte form. Under a solver that lost the
// back edge this would still be two bytes, which is why `diverging` above is
// the half that catches the mistake.
func agreeing @a8 {
.head:
    lda #$01
    sep #$20
    bra .head
}
